# Code owners are requested as reviewers. They grant no permission, and no
# merge waits on them while the rules on main require no code-owner approval.
#
# The last matching line wins, so every line repeats the default owner. The
# @scenario-labs/platform team (maintain access here) joins on release and
# security files, so neither is ever blocked on one person being available.

# Every path
* @hervenivon

# release-please rewrites these three files on every release PR (and, per
# AGENTS.md, nothing else may touch them), so a release PR requests both.
/CHANGELOG.md @hervenivon @scenario-labs/platform
/version.txt @hervenivon @scenario-labs/platform
/.release-please-manifest.json @hervenivon @scenario-labs/platform

# The release machinery itself. A change here redefines what a release is,
# what lands in the notes, or where they are published, so it gets the same
# review as the release PRs it produces.
/release-please-config.json @hervenivon @scenario-labs/platform
/.github/workflows/release-please.yml @hervenivon @scenario-labs/platform
/.github/workflows/publish-changelog.yml @hervenivon @scenario-labs/platform

# Security: the policy, the supply-chain report, dependency updates, and the
# file that decides who reviews them.
/SECURITY.md @hervenivon @scenario-labs/platform
/.github/workflows/scorecard.yml @hervenivon @scenario-labs/platform
/.github/dependabot.yml @hervenivon @scenario-labs/platform
/.github/CODEOWNERS @hervenivon @scenario-labs/platform
